  "output_1": "Aap essentially ek aise planet ke baare mein pooch rahe hain jahan intelligent life hai lekin fossil fuels nahi hain. Yeh thoda plausible lagta hai. Humne (chhote) telescopes bana liye the aur space ke baare mein curious the, usse bahut pehle jab hum fossil fuels ko apne ghar garam karne ke alawa zyada kuch ke liye use karte the. Unhe probably eventually electric vehicles banane mein bahut accha hona padega, including electric planes. Usse pehle, woh alien-ghode ke equivalent gaadiyon mein phase rahenge (lekin shayad electric lights, radios, computers ke saath - yeh interesting ho sakta hai). Mujhe metallurgy ke baare mein zyada nahi pata lekin yeh definitely sochne layak cheez hai - iske liye high temperatures chahiye jo shayad sirf fossil fuels se hi achievable ho. Silicon-based life, Earth ke carbon-based life ke opposite, ek stock sci-fi trope hai jo fossil fuels ki kami aur unhe synthesize karne ki ability na hone ko explain karne mein help kar sakta hai.",
  "output_2": "Aap gravitational potential well ko aur gehra kar sakte hain. Ek planet jiska potential well chaar guna gehra ho, usko orbit mein jaane ke liye hamare rockets se lagbhag 10 guna bade rockets ki zaroorat hogi, yeh assume karte hue ki same rocket motor designs aur fuels use kiye ja rahe hain. Generally, aap planet ko denser ya bada banakar aisa kar sakte hain, haalanki density aur surface gravity ke terms mein (jo shayad ek story ke liye sabse relevant parameters hain), potential depth (surface gravity)^(2)/density ke hisaab se scale karta hai. For example, Neptune ki potential depth Earth se 4 guna zyada hai, zyada tar uski kam density ke kaaran (uski surface gravity Earth ke lagbhag same hai)."
}

